Subject: [PATCH 0/2] MicroBlaze: two relaxation defects in the symbol table

microblaze_elf_relax_section() has two defects in the code that adjusts
symbols after deleting bytes.  Both are independent of the relocation
addend corruption fixed by a639e0adaf2 and f55053f1dc9, and both still
reproduce on current master.  They apply to master with git am in this
order; 2/2 touches lines adjacent to 1/2.

  1/2  microblaze: fix --wrap and relaxation conflict
       Under --wrap, an object that both defines __wrap_SYMBOL and
       refers to SYMBOL has two elf_sym_hashes slots pointing at one
       hash entry, so the relaxation fixup is subtracted from its value
       twice and __wrap_SYMBOL resolves before its own first
       instruction.  RISC-V fixed the same defect as PR ld/22756; this
       copies the guard.
  2/2  microblaze: fix st_size adjustment when relaxing
       The size fixup is measured from the already-lowered st_value,
       so it corrects a span the symbol never occupied and a
       function's size can overlap the next function.

1/2 is the one with a runtime consequence.  DejaGnu's testglue.c defines
__wrap_exit and calls exit; GCC links it into every execution test on a
board that sets needs_status_wrapper, and it writes the "*** EXIT code
N" line that DejaGnu parses to obtain a test's exit status.  GCC's
MicroBlaze LINK_SPEC passes -relax unconditionally, so on this target
__wrap_exit has been resolving into the tail of the preceding function
and no status line is written.  Every execution test is then reported as
status 2, against the board rather than the linker.  Fixing this is what
allowed a DejaGnu board to run gcc.c-torture/execute on MicroBlaze at
all.

2/2 affects st_size only, which is read by debuggers, nm -S and anything
deriving function bounds from the symbol table.  No generated code
changes.  It is sent with 1/2 because it is three lines in the same
hunk.

How to review this ------------------

The bfd change in 1/2 adds 24 lines: 11 of comment taken from another
backend, 10 of code and 3 blank.  The quickest check is to diff it
against the original, ignoring whitespace, since it sits one nesting
level deeper:

    diff -w <(sed -n '923,945p' bfd/elf32-ft32.c) \
            <(sed -n '/wrap SYMBOL. option is causing/,/^	    }$/p' \
                  bfd/elf32-microblaze.c)

Three kinds of difference remain, and nothing else:

  - the loop form.  ft32 walks a sym_hashes pointer; this walks an
    index into elf_sym_hashes, because that is how the surrounding
    MicroBlaze loop is written.  cur_sym_index is a size_t rather than
    a hash pointer for the same reason.
  - the comment is rewrapped to fit the deeper indentation.
  - "the symbols whom been already checked" is corrected to "which have
    already been checked".  Say so if you would rather it were copied
    verbatim.

git log -S "we don't want to adjust" -- bfd/ shows the other four
backends carrying this guard, PR ld/22756 among them.

For 2/2, the question to ask is whether calc_fixup()'s size range should
start at the old or the new value.  bfd/elf32-microblaze.c around line
1805 has calc_fixup(); the second argument is a length, and the loop
sums the deletions between start and start + size.  Reading it that way,
the start has to be where the symbol was.

To reproduce either without a MicroBlaze toolchain, both tests are
self-contained assembly and run from the ld testsuite:

    make check-ld RUNTESTFLAGS=microblaze.exp

That gives 5 passes with the series applied.  Reverting only the
bfd/elf32-microblaze.c hunk of either patch and rebuilding ld fails
exactly that patch's test and leaves the other's passing, which is the
clearest way to see what each one does:

    relax-wrap.d  __wrap_foo links at 0x90000018 instead of 0x9000001c,
                  so its first instruction is the previous function's
                  closing ADDIK r1, r1, 28
    relax-size.d  f's st_size stays 16 where its code is 12 bytes, so it
                  overlaps the next function

Both tests use an explicit linker script so the addresses in the
expected output are fixed.  relax-wrap-support.s and the second section
of relax-size.s hold the branch targets, which have to be in another
section: within one section the assembler resolves the branch itself and
emits no IMM, so there is nothing for relaxation to delete and the test
would check nothing.

Testing -------

ld testsuite on microblaze-elf, master versus the series: 482 expected
passes, 15 expected failures, 0 unexpected failures.  Before the series
the same configuration gives 480 and the two new tests fail.
microblaze.exp alone is 5 passes with the series and 3 with neither
patch.  The three existing relax-addend tests are unchanged throughout.

Both defects are also present in the Xilinx binutils 2.36.1 snapshot
shipped by the RTEMS MicroBlaze toolchain, with the same error
magnitudes.  That was checked by running the two reduced cases through
the installed binaries; I did not build its sources.

I have tested only microblaze.  elf32-sh.c, which these loops were
copied from, has the same shape as 1/2 and no guard, but its narrower
per-deletion window may mask the problem and I have not tested it.

